Hmmm, there's a cliche' in developer lore -

The first version of a program teaches you *what* you want
the application to do.  (Think rogue in this context).

The second version of a program teaches you *how* you
want the application to work, because the rewrite now acts
the way you wanted.  (Think hack in this context).

The third version of a program teaches you that the market
has moved on and the technology is no longer needed,
even though your version now performs the way you wanted.
(Think nethack in this context).

When I read "X11" or "Solaris 10" or "IPv6" I laugh and think
of this old cliche.

When I ponder it against the sourgeforge project entry for
Nethack I understand why the DevTeam has it marked as
stable.  Roguelikes gave rise to some of the best first person
shooter computer games by adding graphics and switching
from map to first person viewpoint, but commercially viable
computer games have long since moved past this point.
Of course we are deliberately discussing a product that has
no intention of being commercially viable ...

